# Carrier Wound #29: The Debug Transcript Filled The Bucket It Wrote To

Wound #28 made a burned-agent-run lane stop wearing the transient
`adapter_unavailable` label. It now classifies a no-marker lane failure by
heartbeat depth and surfaces the EXACT step the lane died after. We shipped it,
deployed it, and re-drove dream-shape runs on the fixed code. Two of them named
the dying step out loud:

```
run-live-20260614T165913566Z-a8bc84dc  →  blocked / planner_lane_incomplete
run-live-20260614T175503461Z-f9a37a09  →  blocked / planner_lane_incomplete
"Agent lane reached step \"build-transcript\" — the agent ran but the lane
 committed no usable result (lane-internal failure, NOT a transport outage)"
```

Wound #28 worked exactly as designed: it refused to guess and handed us the
step. The #28 receipt's own "Follow-ups" deferred the build-transcript fast-cap
to **"do WITH evidence after the next honest run names the dying step, NOT on a
guess."** Two live runs naming `build-transcript` IS that evidence. This is the
carrier finishing the sentence #28 started.

## Mechanism — the transcript writer re-concatenated its own oversized inputs

The `build-transcript` step assembles a human-readable debug transcript. The
original writer was a bare redirect:

```bash
{
  printf '# Pi agent lane transcript\n\n'
  ...
  cat "$raw_output_path"      # the FULL raw agent output, unbounded
  cat "$LANE_OUTPUT_PATH"     # a failed-normalize copy of that same raw output
  cat "$stderr_path"
} > "$LANE_TRANSCRIPT_PATH"
```

On a happy run the raw output is small and this is invisible. On a real dream
run the agent produces a large transcript, and `normalize-output` had ALREADY
concluded `normalized:false` — which leaves `LANE_OUTPUT_PATH` holding a verbatim
copy of the raw output. So the transcript redirect writes the oversized payload
**twice** (raw + its normalize-failure clone), producing a transcript roughly 2×
the raw size while raw + lane_output already occupy ~2× on disk — a transient
peak near 4× the agent's output.

The sandbox is `instance_type: "lite"` — a small disk. The peak hit ENOSPC. The
redirect failed mid-write under `set -e`, and the lane aborted **before
`emit-marker`** — no result marker, no receipt, and (here's the carrier sin) **no
word about disk**. The operator got "reached build-transcript" and had to GUESS
whether it was disk, git, a hash, or an OOM. Wound #28 told us *where*; it could
not tell us *why*, because the lane carried no diagnostic channel that survives an
abort.

> The Steady State pattern says, for every mechanism that accumulates a resource,
> some other mechanism must recycle that resource. […] Any mechanism that
> accumulates resources (whether it is log files in the filesystem, rows in the
> database, or caches in memory) is like the bucket from those high-school
> calculus problems. The bucket fills up at a certain rate […]. It must be
> drained.
>
> — Michael Nygard, *Release It!*, §5.4 Steady State

The transcript is exactly that bucket: a debug artifact that accumulates the full
agent output (twice) with no draining mechanism. The fix bounds the accumulation
AND adds a diagnostic channel that names the disk state even when the write dies.

## Fix — bound the bucket, guard the write, narrate the disk

`cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lane-command.ts`, the `build-transcript` block. The
transcript is DEBUG — never load-bearing; the committed output pin is the source
of truth. So it is now safe to bound and to fail soft.

**Diagnostic channel that survives an abort** (init near line 60, added with #28's
wiring): `diagnostics_path="/workspace/.piwf-lane-diagnostics"`, truncated at lane
start, with a `diag()` helper that appends `>> ... 2>/dev/null || true` (never
fatal). The infra catch in `cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.ts` reads this file
before destroying the sandbox and threads it into
`classifyAgentLaneIncompleteFailure({ diagnostics })`; `AgentLaneIncompleteError`
appends `[lane diagnostics: …]` to its message. Because the operator blocker IS
`error.message`, the disk/size state rides out on the status page — no staring.

**Disk + size telemetry, emitted BEFORE the write** so it exists even if the write
aborts:

```bash
diag "build-transcript disk: $(df -Pk /workspace | awk 'NR==2{print $4" KB free of "$2" KB"}')"
diag "build-transcript raw_output_bytes: $raw_output_bytes"
diag "build-transcript lane_output_bytes: $lane_output_bytes"
diag "build-transcript stderr_bytes: $stderr_bytes"
diag "build-transcript section_cap_bytes: $transcript_section_cap_bytes"
```

**Bounded section writer** — drains the bucket. `emit_capped_section()` writes at
most `transcript_section_cap_bytes` (default 262144) via `head -c`, then appends a
truncation notice naming the real size and pointing at the committed output pin:

```
[transcript truncated: 262144 of <N> bytes shown — the full output is the
 committed output pin, not this debug transcript]
```

Raw Output, Normalized Output, Stderr, and the mounted-packages dump all flow
through the cap. The transcript can no longer exceed ~4 caps regardless of agent
output size.

**Guarded, non-fatal write** — the whole assembly runs under `set +e` … `set -e`
with the byte count captured, and a fallback so the transcript path ALWAYS exists:

```bash
set +e
{ ...sections... } > "$LANE_TRANSCRIPT_PATH" 2>/dev/null
transcript_write_status=$?
set -e
diag "build-transcript write_exit: $transcript_write_status"
if [ ! -f "$LANE_TRANSCRIPT_PATH" ]; then
  printf '[transcript unavailable: build-transcript write failed (exit %s); see lane diagnostics]\n' \
    "$transcript_write_status" > "$LANE_TRANSCRIPT_PATH" 2>/dev/null || true
fi
```

A transcript write can no longer abort the lane. If it fails it leaves a stub and
a `write_exit: <nonzero>` diagnostic; the lane proceeds to `hash-artifacts` and
beyond, so the agent's real work commits instead of being thrown away over a debug
artifact.

### String.raw note (carried, so the next editor doesn't re-trip it)

`buildPiAgentLaneCommand()` is a `String.raw` template, so JS `${...}`
interpolation still fires inside the bash. The original author used ONLY bare
`$var` (never `${VAR:-default}` param expansion) because `${}` collides with JS
interpolation. The cap override therefore defaults under a brief `set +u` /
`set -u` instead of `${PIWF_TRANSCRIPT_SECTION_CAP_BYTES:-262144}`, and every byte
count is a bare-var assignment with an explicit `[ -n "$x" ] || x=unknown` guard.
Two PARSE_ERRORs were paid to learn this; the convention is now load-bearing.

## Chaos tests that would have caught it (hostile doubles, not polite mocks)

`tests/integration/cloudflare-sandbox-agent-lanes.test.ts` — 2 new tests that
SLICE the REAL `build-transcript` block out of `buildPiAgentLaneCommand()`'s
emitted bash (`command.indexOf("mark build-transcript")` →
`command.indexOf("\nmark hash-artifacts")`, throwing if either anchor moves) and
run it under a faithful harness (real `diag()`, real env wiring) against tmpdir
fixtures:

- **Scenario A — oversized output cannot ENOSPC the lane.** Raw + lane outputs are
  4096 bytes each with a 1024-byte cap. Asserts the transcript is bounded
  (`< 4096`), carries the `[transcript truncated:` notice, the diagnostics name
  `lane_output_bytes: 4096` / `raw_output_bytes` / `write_exit: 0`, and the script
  reaches a `POST_TRANSCRIPT_REACHED` sentinel. **Pre-fix:** the bare `cat` redirect
  embeds the full 4096 bytes with no notice and no sizes → every assertion flips
  false.
- **Scenario B — a write that cannot open its target does NOT abort blind.** The
  transcript path is placed under a regular file (`ENOTDIR`, a faithful proxy for
  ENOSPC's open failure). Asserts the diagnostics record `disk:` state and
  `write_exit: <nonzero>`, and the script STILL reaches `POST_TRANSCRIPT_REACHED`.
  **Pre-fix:** the bare redirect under `set -e` aborts → no sentinel, no
  diagnostics.

These are hostile doubles in the franchise sense ([[polite-fakes-franchise-wound]]):
they reproduce the production transport (real emitted bash, real filesystem
failure modes — oversized files, un-openable targets) rather than a politer mock
that would never ENOSPC.

`pnpm typecheck` clean · `pnpm check` clean · `pnpm vitest run` =
**55 files / 426 passed** (incl. the 2 new chaos tests).

## What this does NOT claim (skepticism held)

ENOSPC is the leading hypothesis for a8bc84dc/f9a37a09, strongly supported by the
double-write mechanism on a `lite` disk — but the live runs predate the diagnostic
channel, so we have NOT yet seen `disk: … KB free` from a real abort. This fix is
correct under every candidate cause: bounding + guarding the transcript removes it
as a failure source regardless, and the `diag()` channel will name the TRUE state
on the next run. If the next honest run shows disk is fine and the lane now advances
to `build-receipt` and dies THERE, that is the next wound — `build-receipt` still
does a full `readFileSync(raw_output)`, and we harden it then, with evidence, not on
a guess. We did NOT touch any other step's I/O on speculation.

## The carrier discipline (ninth wound, same shape)

Observability is a carrier property. Wound #28 made the lane say WHERE it died;
Wound #29 makes it say WHY, with the disk numbers in hand, even when the failing
operation is the very write that would have recorded them. The governing demand —
*"feels like we should be able to observe the run in progress and not guess if it's
functioning instead of waiting and staring at it like fucking dummies"* — is
satisfied one wound at a time by refusing to let any step fail mutely. A debug
artifact must never be able to kill the run whose work it was merely describing,
and a resource that accumulates must drain. The bucket now has a hole in the
bottom, and a gauge on the side.
